WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.580
Ground motion can bend, shake, or collapse structures.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.460
Performance depends on design, connections, materials, and the local shaking, not

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.080
simply whether a building is made of one named material.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.880
Loose sediment can amplify motion relative to firm bedrock.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.720
A structure’s natural period can also interact with the shaking, so

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.140
proximity to a fault alone does not explain every damaged building.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.700
Ground rupture can offset roads and foundations.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.820
On steep slopes, shaking can trigger landslides and rock falls that

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.980
threaten sites beyond the immediate fault trace.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.540
Liquefaction occurs when shaking causes loose, water-saturated sediment to lose much

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.220
of its strength. Buildings can settle or tilt as their support

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.940
fails; the sediment has not melted into magma.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.440
Aftershocks follow stress changes caused by the main earthquake.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.020
They can damage structures already weakened by the first shock, even

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.580
when their magnitudes are smaller.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.040
Both foundation conditions and structural condition matter after the main shock.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.020
A building that remains standing is not automatically undamaged or safe.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.160
Broken gas lines can feed fires;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.420
broken water mains can hinder firefighting.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.500
Dam failures and land subsidence can cause flooding.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.120
Underwater displacement can generate a tsunami.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.360
Hazard maps, stronger construction, base isolation, secured utilities, and emergency planning

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.620
can reduce losses. They cannot make an area completely free of

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.740
earthquake risk. Current safety guidance:

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.700
Drop, Cover, and Hold On under sturdy nearby furniture when possible.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.580
The supplied course material includes doorway advice;

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.580
current USGS guidance rejects the doorway as the preferred refuge.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.440
Avoid open flames when gas lines may be damaged.
